"Make the Rust server's systems unit-testable in-process (fast, hermetic) by abstracting the SpacetimeDB touchpoints behind a trait with a real and a mock implementation, generated by the ecs-macro we own. The chokepoint: Ecs<'a> is a Copy wrapper around &ReducerContext that Derefs to it, and every generated accessor bottoms out at ecs.db (a concrete spacetimedb::Local). Two things must be abstracted: (1) TABLE ACCESS -- have the macro generate a Store trait (per-component find/insert/update/delete/iter + the unique-index finders like by-entity_id/by-identity + entity create/delete), with a real impl over &Local and an in-memory HashMap-backed mock; Ecs holds the Store instead of deref-ing to Local. Prefer an ENUM backend (Real(&Local) | Mock(..)) over a generic Ecs<S> so <S> does not thread through EntityHandle and every generated impl -- game-logic signatures stay identical. (2) CONTEXT-DERIVED CAPABILITIES -- timestamp, rng(), sender() currently reach through the Deref to the real ReducerContext; a mock has none, so expose them as injectable on Ecs (fixed timestamp, seeded rng, fake identity). Resolution of the stdb-types tension: KEEP the direct stdb row types (SpacetimeType-derived structs) -- they are plain data the mock stores in maps; it is the ctx/Local BEHAVIOR that must be abstracted, not the types. Also route the ~dozen direct ctx.db.<table>() sites (event.rs, action.rs, ecs_extension.rs, a few in system.rs: entities/traits/baselines/location_maps/observable_events, and the asset loaders) through the Store trait so the mock sees them. This unlocks testing the behavioral edge cases that pure-arithmetic extraction cannot reach: multi-tick damage latency, the early/middle/late event-queue ordering (buff before attack), same-tick accumulation before hp_system nets+clamps, entity_stats dirty-flag recompute + action-set composition, map-gen topology, enemy targeting. Complementary to (and cheaper-per-test than) the real-instance E2E task. Note: the arithmetic kernels themselves (damage max(0,dmg+atk-def), hp/ep clamps) are too trivial to be worth extracting; StatBlock AddAssign's id-vec union/dedup is the one pure unit worth testing without this seam."